**Verdict: revise.** Trust is strained, value is useful, and there's one clear hit. It has **0 blockers** and 7 concerns.

By the end, a reader who doesn't know Druski can say what the article argues and what type it lands on (8w7, with 3 left open as the rival). The weak spots are the opening and the section the whole page is built around.

**Strongest hit:** the Mafiathon split. "The apology went to the boy. The complaint came from his mother, and that one he never answered." It lets a reader with no background decide "is he a good person?" for themselves. The line after it, "This is where the Type 8 frame stops explaining and starts excusing," is where I started trusting the article.

**Highest-priority concern (UNFAMILIAR-C1):** the page never says who Erika Kirk is: the widow of Charlie Kirk, who was killed six months before the sketch. Without that, a reader can't tell why a senator called the sketch "beneath contempt." The defenders get quoted at length, and a "symmetry argument" gets dismissed without ever being stated (C3). A reader who looks her up afterward will think the article left out the heaviest part of the backlash. That's why trust is strained rather than intact. The fix is one appositive in the body and a short gloss in the FAQ.

**Next concern (C2):** the page never says who he is or how he got famous. There's no "sketch comedian" line in the intro or TL;DR. The story also skips from home skits on his mother's sofa straight to Drake's video. One identity sentence and one breakout sentence fix it, paid for by cutting the "big energy / big presence" line. The other five concerns cover:
